---
title: Collecting Events
sidebar:
  order: 6
---

Collecting events help you keep track of activity, effort, personnel, environmental,
and astronomy data for a period of work at a site. Specimen records link to a
collecting event, not directly to a site, so the event is the bridge between a
specimen and the site, coordinate, method, and collector context.

## Setting up events

The NAHPU default list for `Primary activities` is `Collecting`, `Recording`,
and `Observing`. `Other` is deliberately not included, but you can add it or
any protocol-specific activity. Existing activity values remain available even
when they are not in the configured list.

{% steps %}

1. Navigate to the dashboard.
2. Tap/click the hamburger menu in the top left corner.
3. Select `Settings`.
4. Select `Events`.
5. Edit primary activities, collection methods, and personnel roles to match
   your field scenario.
6. Under `Environmental data fields`, select the built-in fields that should
   appear in the event form. The defaults are ambient temperature, ambient
   humidity, cloud cover, rainfall, and notes.

{% /steps %}

## Managing collecting events

Open an event and use its three-dot menu to manage the record:

- `Edit`: change the event fields, effort, personnel, or environmental data.
- `Duplicate event`: copy the site, times, activity, notes, efforts, and
  personnel. The dates move forward one day and the new event gets an empty
  environmental-data record.
- `Show QR`: display the event record for another mobile device to scan.
- `Export event`: save the event and its related data as JSON.
- `Scan QR`: create or replace an event from an event QR code.
- `Import event`: create or replace an event from an exported JSON record.
- `Delete record`: delete the event, its efforts, its event personnel,
  environmental data, and per-event custom values. A specimen record that
  references the event must be moved or deleted first.
- `Delete all records`: delete all collecting events and their related effort,
  personnel, and environmental data in the current project.

When importing an event, choose whether the incoming record creates a new event
or updates an existing event. Confirm the referenced site and personnel after
the import.

Default collection methods include `Hand`, `Net`, and `Pitfall`. Default
personnel roles include `Leader` and `Helper`. You can add more specific values,
such as mist net, harp trap, visual encounter survey, driver, recorder, or
observer, depending on your protocol.

## Creating a new event

{% steps %}
1. Navigate to the event page using the bottom navigation bar.

2. Use the `+` button at the top right corner to create a new event.

    You can also use the three dot menu icon and tap on `Create event`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    You can minimize repeated entry by duplicating an event. Tap/click the
    three-dot menu, then choose `Duplicate event`. NAHPU copies the site, times,
    activity, notes, effort, and personnel, increments the `Start Date` and
    `End Date` by one day, and creates a new empty environmental-data record.

    {% /aside %}

3. Select the `Site ID`.

4. Add the `Start Date` and `End Date`.

    The `Event ID` is generated from `{Site ID}-{Start Date}`.

5. Edit the `Event ID` (optional).

    If you have multiple events with the same `Site ID` and `Start Date`, add an
    ID suffix. This is useful when you separate day and night work, run multiple
    activities at the same site, or need to distinguish repeated events on the
    same date.

6. Add `Start Time` and `End Time`.

7. Add `Primary activity`.

    The defaults are `Collecting`, `Recording`, and `Observing`. `Other` is not
    a default; add it in `Settings` > `Events` if your protocol needs it. For
    many specimen workflows this will be `Collecting`, but create separate
    events when different activities need their own effort, personnel, or
    environmental context.

{% /steps %}

## General information

The `General Information` card stores the core event identity and timing:

- `Event ID`: generated from site ID, start date, and optional suffix.
- `Site ID`: site where the event occurred.
- `Start Date` and `End Date`: event date range.
- `Start Time` and `End Time`: event time range.

For mammal projects, the default start date is set to the previous day and the
default time picker starts at `07:00`, which supports overnight trap checks. For
other catalog formats, date and time pickers default to the current date/time.

## Activity

The `Activity` card stores the broad purpose of the event.

- `Primary activity`: selected from the editable controlled vocabulary in
  event settings. Stored legacy values are also offered so existing data is
  not lost.
- `Notes`: free-text notes about the activity, secondary activities, protocol
  details, or why `Other` was selected.

## Adding effort

{% steps %}

1. Use the `+ Add effort` button to create a new effort.

2. Select a method.

3. Add `Brand and Model` when the method uses specific equipment.

4. Add `Count`.

    This is the number of tools, traps, nets, recorders, or other units used.

5. Add `Size` when applicable.

6. Add `Notes` for effort-specific details.

{% /steps %}

Effort records are stored separately from the event so one event can include
multiple methods. Each effort contains:

- `Method`: user-defined collection method.
- `Brand and Model`: equipment brand/model when relevant.
- `Count`: number of tools or units.
- `Size`: size of the tool or setup.
- `Notes`: any method-specific notes.

Specimen capture records can later select one of these event efforts as the
specimen's `Method`.

## Adding collecting personnel

Use the `Event Personnel` tab to record everyone involved in the event.

{% steps %}

1. Open the personnel tab in the lower right event panel.
2. Use `+ Add personnel`.
3. Select a `Personnel` entry from the project personnel list.
4. Select a `Role`.

{% /steps %}

Personnel roles are user-defined in event settings. Add anyone who contributed
to the event, including collectors, leaders, helpers, drivers, observers,
recorders, or people who assisted with logistics. Specimen capture records can
later select an event personnel entry as the specimen's `Collector`.

## Environmental data and astronomy

NAHPU creates one environmental-data record for each collecting event. In
`Settings` > `Events`, select which built-in fields appear in the form. Hiding
a field changes only the form: previously stored values remain in the database
and in exports.

Available built-in fields include:

- `Day Lowest` and `Day Highest`: daytime temperature range in degrees Celsius.
- `Night Lowest` and `Night Highest`: nighttime temperature range in degrees
  Celsius.
- `Average`: average humidity percentage.
- `Dew Point`: dew point temperature.
- `Cloud cover`: a code from `0` (clear) through `8` (fully overcast), or `9`
  when the sky is obscured. One okta represents one eighth of the visible sky.
- `Rainfall`, `Ambient temperature`, and `Ambient humidity`: conditions at the
  event.
- `Water temperature`, `pH`, `Dissolved oxygen`, and `Flow velocity`: aquatic
  measurements.
- `Sunrise` and `Sunset`: local sunrise and sunset times.
- `Moon Phase`: one of `New Moon`, `Waxing Crescent`, `First Quarter`,
  `Waxing Gibbous`, `Full Moon`, `Waning Gibbous`, `Last Quarter`, or
  `Waning Crescent`.
- `Notes`: additional weather, habitat, or astronomy notes.

Use `Add custom field` or `Manage custom fields` in this section for
installation-wide or project-specific environmental fields. Their values
belong to the individual event, are not filtered by specimen catalog format,
and are included in event, specimen, document, Darwin Core, record-exchange,
and project-transfer exports.

Environmental data is not copied when duplicating an event. The duplicated
event gets a blank record so you can enter the correct conditions for that date.

## How specimen records use events

When you create or edit a specimen, the `Capture Records` card pulls data from
collecting events:

- `Site ID` filters the available event IDs.
- `Event ID` links the specimen to the event.
- `Method` comes from the event's effort records.
- `Collector` comes from the event personnel list.
- `Coordinate ID` comes from coordinates associated with the event site.

Changing a specimen's event clears event-dependent fields such as method,
collector, and coordinate, while preserving the specimen's capture date and time.
